2025-04-26 00:24:21,246 - walden_rag - INFO - Data from vectore DB: '[{'_additional': {'certainty': 0.7216581106185913, 'distance': 0.5566838}, 'doc_id': '6-1-chunk-6', 'source': '', 'text': ' She was also a school administrator for a number of years and has the unique background of both an educator and a human resource practitioner.\n\nCourses Taught\nHRMG 4201 - Strategic Human Resource Management\n\nHRMG 3001 - Human Resource Management\n\nHRMG 4203 - HRM: Analysis & Problems\n\nHRMG 4202 - Human Resource Development & Change\n\nEducation\nBS, University of Central Floridsa\n\nMA, Webster University\n\nMS, Walden University\n\nEdS, NovaSotheastern University\n\nEdD, University of Sarasota/Argosy\n\nPublic Service\nAustin City Limits , Volunteer - Austin\n\nAwards / Honors\nOutstanding Principal Award, Eanes ISD, 2001\n\nPublications\nLynch, S. (2011). Answering the Critics of Online Education. www.NAS.org', 'url': 'https://academics.waldenu.edu/catalog/management-human-potential/undergraduate/bachelor-science-business-administration', 'url_path': '/online-bachelors-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.7166395783424377, 'distance': 0.56672084}, 'doc_id': '6-1-chunk-5', 'source': '', 'text': " Students progress through the Tempo Learning® model by successfully passing competency assessments. A student must successfully achieve or master all competency assessments to graduate from the program.\n\nStudents earn the same Walden degree whether they choose the course-based or the competency-based modality.\n\nDegree Requirements\n181 total quarter credit hours\nGeneral Education courses (45 credits)\nCore competencies (61 credits) or\nAIM core competencies (66 credits)\nElective courses (70 credits) or\nAIM elective courses (65 credits)\nCapstone competency (5 credits)\n BS in Business Administration (General Program)\n Accelerate Into Master's\nNote on Curriculum\nIn an effort to ensure competencies are kept up to date and relevant to meet the learning outcomes of the program, the number and subject matter of individual competencies needed to satisfy program course requirements may change. However, program outcomes and the number and subject matter of courses will not be impacted. As a result, the required competencies listed in a student’s individual learning plan may differ from what is currently listed in the catalog. Faculty of this program Shannon Lynch\nContributing Faculty. College of Management and Technology\nB.S. Business Administration (BSBA).  Dr. Shannon Lynch is a Contributing Faculty Member in the College of Management & Human Potential. Dr. Lynch has served in various capacities during her time at Walden which has spanned over 21 years. She has experience and interest in Human Resources as a certified HR Professional through SHRM. She was also a school administrator for a number of years and has the unique background of both an educator and a human resource practitioner.\n\nCourses Taught\nHRMG 4201 - Strategic Human Resource Management\n\nHRMG 3001 - Human Resource Management", 'url': 'https://academics.waldenu.edu/catalog/management-human-potential/undergraduate/bachelor-science-business-administration', 'url_path': '/online-bachelors-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.7151343822479248, 'distance': 0.56973124}, 'doc_id': '3-2-chunk-3', 'source': 'walden_university', 'text': "uhainy, B. (2021). Ways to Bloom Where You’re Planted. Information Security Professional ISC2\n\nPresentations\nDUHAINY, B. (2022). Cybersecurity Attacks and Countermeasures . FBI Citizens Academy\n\nDuhainy, B. (2022). Infrastructure Security. FBI Citizens Academy Duhainy, B. (2020). Cybersecurity in the age of AI, Automation and GDPR. Walden University. Gary Griffith\nAcademic Coordinator  \nCollege of Management and Human Potential\nDoctor of Business Administration (DBA)   Dr. Gary Griffith started teaching as a contributing faculty for Walden University in the Master of Science in Information Systems Management program in 2011. In 2012 I accepted a core faculty position in the Master of Science in Information Technology program. Since starting my career at Walden University I have taught 16 different courses in BSIT, MISM, MSIT and DIT programs. Most of the courses I teach are in Walden University's Health Informatics, Software Engineering, and Cyber Security Master's degree programs. I performed the research which enabled Walden University to be designated as an NSA Center in Academic Excellence in Cyber Defense in 2014. I was part of a team working closely with leadership to design and implement new master’s degree specialization in Big Data Analytics. Recently I have started helping students in the DIT program where I am the dissertation chair and committee member.\nPrior to coming to Walden University, I worked in the field of information systems and technology for over 30 years.", 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-masters-programs/ms-in-cybersecurity', 'url_path': '/online-masters-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.7073076963424683, 'distance': 0.5853846}, 'doc_id': '1-2-chunk-1', 'source': 'walden_university', 'text': "in data science online program will be prepared to: Evaluate emerging technical developments that apply to data science. Analyze current technologies that provide practical solutions to data science problems. Explore the role of supporting technologies for data science in data-driven decision-making. Analyze legal, ethical, professional, and social issue elements within the domain of data science. Differentiate how the techniques and tools of big data predictive analytics can be used to add 'business value' in data-driven decision-making in the modern workplace. Below are some of the distinguished faculty members who bring diverse industry expertise and a passion for teaching to the Walden community. faculty name Gail Miles Program Director Dr. Miles taught Computer Science face-to-face for over 25 years starting at the University of Florida. She retired from Lenoir-Rhyne University as Professor Emerita in 2013. She taught IT online since 2000 in a variety of levels and programs. She was the Director of Online Studies for the Masters Programmes of the Department of Computer Science, University of Liverpool in the UK from 2013 to 2019. She is currently the Associate Dean of Masters programs in Information Technology, Doctor of Information Technology, PhD in Management, and Doctor of Business Administration in College of Management and Human Potential for Walden University\nAll of her graduate work was completed at the University of Florida (MEd, EdS, PhD, and MS). The second faculty name Osama Morad Contributing Faculty College of Management and Human Potential Doctor of Information Technology (DIT). Data science is the process of extracting insights from data.", 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-masters-programs/ms-in-data-science', 'url_path': '/online-masters-programs/ms-in-data-science'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.6951167583465576, 'distance': 0.6097665}, 'doc_id': '5-0-chunk-2', 'source': '', 'text': ' learning track offers support, tuition savings, and resources for those without previous college credit.Career Resources\n\nTake advantage of our Career Planning and Development Center for one-on-one guidance, networking events, essential job search tools, and more.Applying is Easy\n\nNo application fee, essay, or SAT or ACT score is required for admission. Bachelor’s Programs BS in Business Administration, BS in Communication.  An Accredited University\nQuality matters—especially when it comes to your education. Walden is accredited by The Higher Learning Commission and committed to maintaining the highest standards of excellence. Flexibility to Fit Your Schedule\nAt Walden, we are dedicated to offering education options designed to fit the needs of working professionals like you. That is why we offer a wide range of learning options, including a standard, course-based experience, and Tempo Learning®. COURSE BASED\nInstructor-led\nEngage in a course-by-course experience, where you progress on a predetermined schedule. This format allows you to learn alongside your peers, participating in weekly discussions and meeting established deadlines for assignments. Your cost is based on how many credits you need to complete your program.\n\nIdeal for students who are newer to online education and prefer structure, working with others, and having set deadlines.\n\nStarts on May 26\n\nRegister and pay by course.\n\nCourse lengths are predetermined, based on program.\n\nFaculty guide you through each course.\n\nScheduled due dates allow you make steady progress.', 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-bachelors-programs', 'url_path': '/online-bachelors-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.6817291975021362, 'distance': 0.6365416}, 'doc_id': '6-1-chunk-7', 'source': '', 'text': ' Limits , Volunteer - Austin\n\nAwards / Honors\nOutstanding Principal Award, Eanes ISD, 2001\n\nPublications\nLynch, S. (2011). Answering the Critics of Online Education. www.NAS.org\n\nPresentations\nLynch, S. (2016). Special Education in the Public School. Austin Community College. Next faculty is Harry Kemp\nContributing Faculty \nCollege of Management and Technology\nB.S. Business Administration (BSBA)\nDr. Harry Kemp is a Walden University professor and SCORE mentor with extensive expertise in business management. As a former firefighter and author of "How Small Businesses Survived Natural Disasters in Northeastern Florida," Dr. Kemp has valuable insight into disaster management and preparedness for small businesses. He has also been recognized as a Frank Dilley Award winner for his significant contributions to Walden University\'s mission.\nDr. Kemp is an accredited Financial Analyst (AFA) designation from the Global Academy of Finance and Management (GAFM), Dr. Kemp brings specialized knowledge and expertise in financial analysis to his work as a professor and mentor. His multifaceted background and experience as a SCORE mentor make him a valuable resource for entrepreneurs and small business owners seeking to grow and overcome challenges. Dr. Kemp\'s dedication to education, research, and professional development make him a distinguished professional in his field.\n\nEducation\nDBA, Walden University\n\nMBA, Jacksonville University\n\nBS, Edward Waters University\n\nAS, Florida State College at Jacksonville\n\nAA, Florida State College at Jacksonville\n\nPresentations\nKemp, H. A. (2019). The Survival of Small Businesses in Northeastern Florida After A Natural Disaster . Newport Restoration Foundation (NRF)', 'url': 'https://academics.waldenu.edu/catalog/management-human-potential/undergraduate/bachelor-science-business-administration', 'url_path': '/online-bachelors-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.6785895526409149, 'distance': 0.6428209}, 'doc_id': '3-0-chunk-4', 'source': 'walden_university', 'text': 'nsics (5 credits)\nMCYB 6255 - Cybercrime Prevention and Protection (5 credits)\nMCYB 6635 - Secure Coding (5 credits)   Course Sequence\nCourse Sequence\nThe Recommended course sequence is as follows:\n\nQuarters\tCourse\tCredits\nQuarter 1\tMCYB 6005 - The Global Technology Environment\t5 credits\nQuarter 2\tMCYB 6215 - Security Risk Management \t5 credits\nMCYB 6210 - Cloud Computing \t5 credits\nQuarter 3\tMCYB 6190 - Foundations of Intelligent Systems \t5 credits\nMCYB 6670 - Security Engineering and Compliance \t5 credits\nQuarter 4\tMCYB 6735 - Applied Cryptography \t5 credits\nMCYB 6270 - Cyber Forensics \t5 credits\nQuarter 5\tMCYB 6255 - Cybercrime Prevention and Protection \t5 credits\nMCYB 6635 - Secure Coding \t5 credits', 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-masters-programs/ms-in-cybersecurity', 'url_path': '/online-masters-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.6766332387924194, 'distance': 0.6467335}, 'doc_id': '3-2-chunk-1', 'source': 'walden_university', 'text': "Meet Your Academic Team\nMeet some of the distinguished MS in Cybersecurity faculty members who bring diverse expertise and a passion for business to our student community. Robert Duhainy\nCore Faculty  College of Management and Human Potential\nDoctor of Information Technology (DIT)  Dr. Duhainy has 30 years of experience in designing and implementing wireless and cellular Networks and 29 years in technology and computer security. He holds a Masters degree in Telecommunications Electrical Engineering Department at Southern Methodist University and a Ph.D. from Walden University's Information Systems Management program. Dr. Duhainy teaches various courses in Data Communications and Computer Security online. For his Ph.D. dissertation, he worked on developing the most effective approach for deploying a context- and location-aware network, which is particularly important to the U.S. government as it will allow hands-off processing and tracking medical supplies and weaponry. His research focused on RFID deployment methods, minimizing the required number of passive and active tags, and the power requirements needed to operate the networks. His research includes using software agents to safeguard computer networks from attack. He is also involved in various security-related research projects, including Internet of Things(IoT), Blockchain Technologies; Secure Coding; Artificial Intelligence (AI); Cloud Security; Social Engineering; Mobile 5G security; Firewalls and nefarious code detection, and system vulnerability assessments. He received training from the National Security Agency (NSA), Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI), United States Secret Service (USSS), Central Intelligence Agency (CIA), Director of National Intelligence (DNI), and Department of Homeland Security (DHS) on various topics.", 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-masters-programs/ms-in-cybersecurity', 'url_path': '/online-masters-programs/'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.6748346090316772, 'distance': 0.6503308}, 'doc_id': '1-2-chunk-2', 'source': 'walden_university', 'text': " University of Florida (MEd, EdS, PhD, and MS). The second faculty name Osama Morad Contributing Faculty College of Management and Human Potential Doctor of Information Technology (DIT). Data science is the process of extracting insights from data. Using computer algorithms along with their own analytical skills, data scientists look for previously unrecognized connections between data points and use those connections to help improve efficiencies, target consumers, develop new products, and/or augment business strategies. It's a field made possible by a connected, computerized world and it's a field that's growing. Earning an MS in Data Science degree from Walden can help you gain valuable knowledge and skills in your field. Some of the most crucial skill sets include: Communication:Individuals need to know how to communicate their key findings with stakeholders to ensure their work is effective. Refining their communication style helps data scientists formulate what business insights are most important and how they can use data to create a unique snapshot of their customers. Collaboration:Data scientists bring significant value to organizations, but they must collaborate effectively with other departments to be successful. Involving stakeholders in a variety of departments gives data scientists a well-rounded view of the organization. Predictive Analytics:Organizations use predictive analytics in many different ways, including data mining and artificial intelligence algorithms to update and optimize business decisions. A data scientist can offer insights with predictive analytics to make informed and educated decisions to benefit the organization. Problem-Solving:A key skill in driving important business decisions is the ability to view a challenge from many perspectives. This approach allows data scientists to understand the issue they're trying to resolve and use their skills and tools to address and drive good business decisions.", 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-masters-programs/ms-in-data-science', 'url_path': '/online-masters-programs/ms-in-data-science'}, {'_additional': {'certainty': 0.674051821231842, 'distance': 0.65189636}, 'doc_id': '4-0-chunk-2', 'source': 'walden_university', 'text': ' Learn\nIn the Public Management and Leadership specialization you can choose from two degree completion options that are both designed for working professionals.\n\nThe course-based format may be a good option for you if you prefer an instructor-led approach and learning on a predetermined schedule.\n\nTempo Learning® is an alternative way to earn your Walden degree. It’s different from our standard course-based learning because you can work at a pace that makes sense for you, and progress once you’ve shown you understand a subject. With Tempo Learning®, you have the flexibility of online learning, plus the ability to personalize and control the length, cost, and pacing of your program. General Program Specialization\n48 quarter credits\nFoundation course (3 cr.)\nCore courses (25 cr.)\nSpecialization (15 cr.)\nCapstone course (5 cr.) . COMPLETE CURRICULUM PLAN. Walden University’s MS in Criminal Justice program will prepare students to effectively work within criminal justice agencies, governmental organizations, and the private sector. The programs comprise a core of contemporary theory and practice as well as the study of national and international issues in the administration of criminal justice. This includes the intersections of management of local, state, and federal criminal justice systems. Thus, it provides the ability to practically apply knowledge and skills that will impact society. With a unique blend of criminal behavioral theory, human services, technology, homeland security, and management, the program will provide students with critical 21st-century skills.\n\nNote: The MS in Criminal Justice program is offered in two different learning modalities: the course-based modality and the competency-based modality, Tempo Learning®.\n\nLearning Outcomes\nAt the end of the program, students will be able to:', 'url': 'https://www.waldenu.edu/online-masters-programs/ms-in-criminal-justice', 'url_path': '/online-masters-programs/'}]' >>>>>>>>>>>>>>
